    "insert": "Summary:\n\nIn your expose, you state that in the current education system, the focus lies on\ngrading mistakes. And you want to show a new way with LLMs and how these models\ncan help to uncover misconceptions and give students a better understanding of\ntheir mistakes.\n\nTo answer your research questions, you would like to conduct a literature\nreview as an AI-driven error analysis as a first step. As a second step, you\nmay want to fine-tune a model or use prompts to adjust an existing model. With\nthese now prepared LLMs, you want to run experiments with students and evaluate\nthe models output with educators to answer your research questions.\n\nHook/Anker/Teaser:\n\nYou begin your expose with a shocking statement and a possible solution, which\nis an effective way to catch the reader's interest. However, you should provide\nproof for this statement by including a citation.\n\nYou also briefly discuss the domain of the problem and convincingly highlight\nthe relevance of your research topic. Your conclusion about the gap in the\nfield is reasonable and achievable in the limited time frame.\n\nResearch questions:\n\nYour research questions are well tailored to the problem you outline in the\nbeginning of your motivation. They are clear and reasonable, achievable in the\ntime frame.\n\nSota/Framework:\n\nAt the end of your SOTA, you mention that your study will evaluate the\neffectiveness of LLMs in \"diverse subjects\" and \"explore computational\napproaches\". This wording is rather vague. \"Diverse subjects\" could be\ninterpreted broadly, and attempting to cover all available subjects may be\nimpractical, especially in the limited time frame. It's better to focus on a\nfew subjects to still provide a diverse overview while keeping the scope\nmanageable. Similarly, the phrase \"explore computational approaches\" needs\nclarification which approaches you plan to use.\n\nIn your framework you correctly state the theories you plan to use, and both of\nthem are reasonable concepts. One of which is \"Explainable AI\". It is needed to\neffectively evaluate the responses. You also acknowledge that the explanations\nmay be too technical for the educators and students to understand. Your plan to\nprovide \"user-friendly explanations or visual aids\" is a great solution to make\nthe results more accessible.\n\nYou should clarify this sentence: \"This research leverages diagnostic learning\nanalytics, which integrates data-driven methods with cognitive science to\nanalyze student performance.\". The meaning of this sentence is unclear to me.\nIt would be helpful to explain what \"diagnostic learning analytics\" and\n\"data-driven methods with cognitive science\" are and how they are integrated\ninto your approach. This should provide a clearer understanding of how you plan\nto analyze student performance.\n\nMethodology:\n\nIn your methodology, you mention that you will use precision and recall to\npinpoint specific misconceptions and errors. My current understanding is that\nprecision is \"How many retrieved items are relevant?\" and recall is \"How many\nrelevant items are retrieved?\". These are performance metrics that are commonly\nused in object detection and classification in machine learning. However, it is\nunclear how you intend on use them for pinpointing student misconceptions and\nerrors. You should clarify how precision and recall are relevant in this\ncontext or if they are being used differently here.\n\nYou also mention exploring fine-tuning or targeted prompts. I would advise you\nagainst fine-tuning, as this requires access to the model's data, which is\ntypical not available for SOTA AI models that would be most relevant to\nresearch. While there are models that offer access to fine-tuning, they are\ngenerally not comparably to the SOTA models. Training a custom model would be\nout of scope of this paper, as this would also be not comparable to the current\nSOTA models. Therefore, I recommend focusing on targets prompts for exiting\nmodel instead.\n\nNext, you mention handling linguistic diversity with custom prepossessing\npipelines. It would be helpful to explain what these pipelines will do and why\nthey are the best approach to address this problem.\n\nYou mention to use cloud-based systems to process the generated data. But you\ndon't address how this will be financed or plan to handle risks. A potential\nrisk could be data privacy or unavailability due to a vendor error.\n\nThe target demographic is more general, and there is nothing wrong with that.\nHowever, you should mention how you plan to handle outliers, such as a low\nnumber of participants from a specific context, which may not provide enough\ndata for a meaningful analysis. You also don't address the risk of not having\nenough students sign up to your study. You should provide a plan in case this\nhappens, including where and how you plan to recruit them. The same applies to\nthe educators.\n\nLanguage:\n\nYour expose is well written. But you tend to repeat yourself. Especially in\nyour motivation and the beginning of the approach. Reducing this will help to\nmake your arguments clearer and more convincing.\n\nSchedule:\n\nYour schedule looks good and achievable in the time frame, except from the one\nweak where you plan to recruit educator and students and conduct the LLM\nexperiments. This seems too short. I recommend spreading this phase out over\nmore weeks to ensure enough time for proper recruitment and experiments.\n\n"
  }
]